            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TX6-GlowUp</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.4–6.0 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7 hr</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,399, ships</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR1200Pro Glow-Up</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.4–4.0 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">330 lb (LifeSpan)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 hr</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,199, pre-order</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TR5000B Pro+ Glow-Up</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.4–4.0 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb (LifeSpan)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">9 hr</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,699, pre-order</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">InMovement Unsit</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.3–2.0 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb (both sellers)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$2,595</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">MERACH W50 TrekPad</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.6–4.0 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$299.99</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>TX6 or TR1200?</strong> The TR1200 has the same belt width, a lower step-up and a
            $200 lower price, and suits a walker of average build who stays under 4 mph. The TX6 adds
            70 lb of rated capacity, an hour of rated daily use and 2 mph of top speed, and it was
            actually in stock. For a heavier user, the TX6 is the safer LifeSpan.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>TX6 or TR5000B Pro+?</strong> The TR5000B claims the same 400 lb, nine hours a day
            and a 5.0 HP peak motor, for $300 more, but it stops at 4 mph and its own page describes the
            deck as both 0.7 and 1 inch thick. We could not cross-check its figures against an
            independent source, so it is not on a card here. If you need nine hours of rated use, it is
            the one to ask LifeSpan about.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>TX6 or Unsit?</strong> The Unsit is 30 inches wide and built for slow all-day
            walking, but costs about $1,200 more and stops at 2 mph. Our guides to
            <a href="/inmovement-treadmill-desk/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">InMovement’s Unsit</a>
            and the <a href="/imovr-unsit-treadmill-desk/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iMovR Unsit bundle</a>
            cover it. <strong>TX6 or a budget pad?</strong> The MERACH W50 matches the 400 lb rating
            at a fifth of the price, with a narrower, shorter belt and two years of cover rather than
            ten. For an hour a day it is the better value; for six hours a day, the TX6’s deck and
            warranty earn their cost. Our
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads for heavy people</a>
            roundup compares both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy the LifeSpan TX6-GlowUp</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy the TX6 if</strong> you will walk at a desk for several hours most working
            days, you weigh more than about 250 lb or are tall enough that a 40-inch belt feels short,
            and you want a deck wide enough that you stop thinking about where your feet are. It is
            also a reasonable choice for a shared office, where several people of different sizes will
            use one machine, because its rated capacity and daily-use figure leave margin for that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy something else if</strong> you walk for an hour or less a day, weigh under
            about 220 lb, or want to jog, fold the machine away or walk on an incline. A good budget pad
            does an hour a day at a fraction of the price, and a folding treadmill does running. Our
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under desk treadmills</a>
            guide and the
            <a href="/best-treadmill-desks/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill desks</a>
            roundup cover the wider field.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Either way</strong>, buy at LifeSpan’s sale price rather than a reference price,
            check your desk height against a 5.8-inch step-up, plan a non-GFCI outlet, and read the
            return terms before you order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the LifeSpan TX6-GlowUp a good under desk treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On its published figures and the independent sources we checked, yes, for long daily desk walking by heavier or taller users. LifeSpan rates it for 400 lb and seven hours a day, with a 20-inch belt, 0.4 to 6 mph and a 10-year frame warranty. Its weight, step-up height and motor figures differ between sources. We have not tested it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the LifeSpan TX6 and the TX6-GlowUp?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We found no difference. LifeSpan sells one model, the TX6-GlowUp, in black or white; TX6 is the model and part number and Amazon’s listing name. GlowUp is LifeSpan’s name for its current generation of desk treadmills and appears on the TR1200Pro and TR5000B Pro+ too.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does the LifeSpan TX6-GlowUp cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 5 November 2026 LifeSpan listed it at $1,399, down from $1,499, and its dealer 360 Fitness Superstore also showed $1,399. An independent tracker recorded $1,299 at LifeSpan and Amazon in February 2026, with a $150 Amazon delivery charge. Prices change often.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit of the LifeSpan TX6?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                LifeSpan gives a maximum user weight of 400 lb, and its dealer, an independent tracker and the Amazon listing title give the same figure. LifeSpan also rates it for up to seven hours of use a day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How fast does the LifeSpan TX6 go?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                LifeSpan gives a speed range of 0.4 to 6.0 mph using the included remote. With LifeSpan’s optional Omni Hub desk console, its dealer and an independent tracker say the top speed is limited to 4 mph.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug the LifeSpan TX6 into any outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                LifeSpan specifies a dedicated 115 V circuit with a standard NEMA 5-15 outlet and says not to plug it into a GFCI outlet, because it may trip. Check the outlet at your desk before delivery, especially in a garage, basement or office.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a LifeSpan TX6</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The TX6 is a straightforward machine to buy well. These are the errors that cost buyers money or a working setup.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging it by an Amazon star rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Amazon listing shares reviews with older LifeSpan models, some written years before the TX6 existed. Read the TX6-specific reviews, or ignore the average and rely on the published figures and the return window.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning desk height from the lowest step-up figure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sources give 4.6, 5 and 5.8 inches. Plan from 5.8 and add about six inches to your usual standing-desk height. A desk that stops an inch short makes the whole setup uncomfortable.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging it into a GFCI outlet or a power strip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">LifeSpan says the TX6 may trip a GFCI outlet and wants a dedicated circuit. Check the outlet near your desk before delivery day, and do not run it from a surge strip or extension cord.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying the $1,899 reference price, or ignoring delivery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">LifeSpan and its dealer both showed $1,399 on 5 November 2026, and Amazon has charged a delivery fee on top. Compare delivered totals, and treat any discount from $1,899 as no discount at all.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

